 ## Overview
 
-This system generates a personalized home feed by **materializing timelines only where it pays off**. The core insight is to treat a user’s feed as a **cache of candidate items**, not the source of truth: every post is always durable in an **author outbox**, while the **home inbox** is selectively precomputed for the subset of reader–writer pairs where fan-out is efficient.
+This system serves a personalized home feed by treating the home timeline as a **cache of candidates** backed by a durable **per-author outbox**. The single rule that keeps costs bounded is: **push for normal authors, pull for celebrities**.
 
-The design is a hybrid of fan-out-on-write and fan-out-on-read, but not as a vague “mix both”. It uses a crisp partition: **push from normal authors to active readers**, and **pull from high-fanout (“celebrity”) authors at read time**. This avoids catastrophic write amplification while keeping p95 feed latency low for the users who actually read feeds frequently.
+Most posts are pushed into per-reader inboxes asynchronously. Celebrity posts are never fanned out; they are fetched from celebrity outboxes at read time, with hard per-request budgets. The feed endpoint merges, filters, and does lightweight ranking.
 
 ## What Makes This Hard
 
-Naive fan-out-on-write fails when a small number of authors have massive follower graphs: one post triggers millions of writes, blows up queues, and creates hot partitions in timeline storage. Naive fan-out-on-read fails for active readers because every refresh becomes a multi-way merge across hundreds/thousands of followed authors, which is slow and expensive at scale.
+Follower graphs are skewed. Pure push melts down on celebrities (millions of writes per post). Pure pull melts down on active readers (merging thousands of sources).
 
-The trap is trying to “solve ranking” first. The real hard part is **controlling work placement**: deciding which edges of the follow graph get precomputed, how to paginate a feed that is partially materialized, and how to stay operationally sane when follow/unfollow changes and celebrity traffic spikes.
+The real work is **work placement + pagination**: deciding where computation happens and making scrolling stable while new posts arrive, deletes happen, and edges change.
 
